[Event "Imbalanced endgames: the bishop that cannot run"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/BfuMfBOr"]
[FEN "8/8/6p1/8/7p/3K4/7R/3kb3 b - - 3 55"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/6p1/8/7p/3K4/7R/3kb3_b_-_-_3_55"]

{Welcome to imbalanced endgames! The two sides have different pieces, so counting points will not tell you who is winning: what counts is whether each piece can do its job.}
55... g5 {He pushes his g pawn to guard the h pawn, a mistake: his bishop on e1 needed that move to escape to g3. Your king covers the squares in front of his king, so a rook check from a1 is mate. Send the rook to a2. [%cal Yh2a2][%csl Rc2,Rd2,Re2]} 56. Ra2 {The rook threatens mate on a1, and on a2 it is out of reach of a single step by his king. [%cal Ga2a1]} (56. Rxh4 {Grabbing the pawn on h4 walks into his pawn on g5, which guards that square.} 56... gxh4 {Your rook is gone for one pawn, and a bare king cannot stop his bishop and pawn. [%cal Rg5h4]}) (56. Rd2+ {The check puts your rook on d2, right next to his bishop on e1.} 56... Bxd2 {His bishop takes the rook, and his bishop and pawns now beat your lone king. [%cal Re1d2]}) (56. Rc2 {On c2 the rook threatens nothing, so his bishop gets a free move to escape.} 56... Bg3 {The bishop is out on g3, and your rook cannot catch it and both pawns: a draw. [%csl Rg3]}) (56. Rb2 {The rook on b2 threatens mate too, but his king can attack it with one step to c1.} 56... Kc1 {His king chases the rook away, and that lost move lets the bishop out: a draw. [%cal Rc1b2]}) (56. Re2 {The rook attacks the bishop on e1, but his king on d1 guards it. [%cal Rd1e1]} 56... Bc3 {The bishop slips out to c3. Your king cannot take it there, because his king would then take your rook on e2: a draw. [%csl Rc3]}) (56. Rh1 {The rook pins the bishop to his king along the first row, but his king still guards it. [%cal Rd1e1]} 56... g4 {His pawns start running while your rook is busy with the pin: a draw. [%cal Rg4g3]}) 56... Kc1 {His king steps to c1 to stop the mate. Keep your rook far away and check him from a1. [%cal Ya2a1]} 57. Ra1+ (57. Rc2+ {Checking from c2 lets his king step away to b1.} 57... Kb1 {His king is safe on b1, and the bishop gets out next move: a draw. [%csl Re1]}) (57. Rb2 {The rook on b2 stands right next to his king, which simply takes it.} 57... Kxb2 {Your rook is gone, and with it the game: you are lost. [%cal Rc1b2]}) (57. Ra3 {Waiting on a3 gives his bishop the free move it needs.} 57... Bg3 {The bishop escapes to g3, and the win escapes with it: a draw. [%csl Rg3]}) (57. Ke2 {Your king attacks the bishop from e2, and the bishop simply runs.} 57... Bg3 {Out it goes to g3, and with your king gone from d3 there is no box left: a draw. [%csl Rg3]}) 57... Kb2 {His only square was b2, where his king attacks your rook. The rook has to move anyway, so let it take the bishop on e1. [%cal Ya1e1]} 58. Rxe1 {The bishop is yours. Now watch your rook deal with his two pawns. [%csl Ge1][%tail]} 58... h3 59. Rh1 59... g4 60. Rg1 60... h2 61. Rg2+ {[%cal Gg2b2,Gg2h2]} 61... Ka3 62. Rxh2 62... Kb4 63. Rg2 63... Kc5 64. Rxg4 {Both pawns are gone, and your rook wins. His bishop was lost because it had no room to run beside its own king. [%csl Gg4]} 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: the rook in the net"]
[Result "0-1"]
