[FEN "7R/8/5p2/4nkp1/7p/5P2/6K1/8 w - - 6 58"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7R/8/5p2/4nkp1/7p/5P2/6K1/8_w_-_-_6_58"]

{Your knight and three pawns against his rook and pawn. His rook has wandered deep into your half, far from its king, and a rook out there can be hunted.}
58. Rh6 {He attacks your pawn on f6, which your king guards anyway. That is his mistake: on h6 the rook is running out of squares.} 58... Nf7 {The knight hits the rook on h6 and guards h8 as well. [%cal Gf7h6,Gf7h8]} (58... Nxf3 {Grabbing the pawn on f3 puts the knight right next to his king.} 59. Kxf3 {His king takes the knight, and your three pawns cannot hold off his rook: you are lost. [%cal Rg2f3]}) (58... Ng4 {The knight on g4 lands where his f pawn can take it.} 59. fxg4+ {His pawn takes the knight with check, and his rook beats your three pawns: you are lost. [%cal Rf3g4]}) (58... Ng6 {The knight on g6 does not attack the rook, so the rook has all the time it needs.} 59. Kf2 {His king walks out and his rook is free again: the position is level.}) (58... h3+ {The pawn check on h3 does nothing to the rook.} 59. Kg3 {His king steps up beside your pawn, and the rook is loose again: level. [%csl Rh3]}) (58... Kf4 {Your king marches to f4 and leaves your pawn on f6 alone.} 59. Rxf6+ {His rook takes the pawn with check and escapes: a draw. [%cal Rh6f6]}) 59. Rh7 59... Kg6 {Now the rook is trapped: every square it can reach is covered by your king or your knight. [%csl Rh7]} (59... Ne5 {Moving the knight off f7 opens the cage.} 60. Ra7 {The rook runs free along the seventh row: the position is level.}) (59... h3+ {The pawn check on h3 hands the rook a pawn to take on its way out.} 60. Rxh3 {His rook takes the pawn and escapes down the h column: a draw. [%cal Rh7h3]}) (59... Nh6 {The knight on h6 blocks the rook's path, but nothing guards it there.} 60. Rxh6 {His rook takes the knight, and nothing traps it any more: you are lost. [%cal Rh7h6]}) (59... Kf4 {Your king runs to f4, away from the knight on f7 that his rook attacks.} 60. Rxf7 {His rook takes the knight for free, and your pawns cannot hold off a rook: you are lost. [%cal Rh7f7]}) (59... g4 {Pushing the g pawn ignores the attack on your knight on f7.} 60. Rxf7 {His rook takes the knight, and the net is gone: you are lost.}) 60. Rxh4 {He gives the rook up for a pawn rather than lose it for nothing.} 60... gxh4 {A rook for one pawn, and your knight and two pawns now win. A rook far from home can be trapped: your knight took its squares and your king closed the net. [%csl Gh4]} (60... Ne5 {Leaving his rook alive lets him save it.} 61. Rh1 {His rook gets back to h1, and your knight and pawns cannot beat it: a draw.}) (60... Kg7 {Stepping aside with the king lets the rook walk away.} 61. Rh1 {The rook is safe on h1, and the win has gone: a draw.}) (60... g4 {Pushing your g pawn instead of taking the rook leaves it hanging on g4.} 61. Rxg4+ {His rook takes the pawn with check, and now you are the one who is lost. [%cal Rh4g4]}) 0-1


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: lure the rook onto the diagonal"]
