[FEN "8/8/6P1/8/4k1BK/r3p3/6P1/8 b - - 0 55"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/6P1/8/4k1BK/r3p3/6P1/8_b_-_-_0_55"]

{Your bishop and two pawns against his rook and pawn. Your pawn on g6 is close to home, but a rook watching it from the side can hold the draw.}
55... Ra5 {His rook swings over to a5, and that is a mistake. There is a win for you now, but only one order of moves gets there.} 56. g7 (56. Bf3+ {The bishop check comes one move early: his rook is not on the diagonal yet.} 56... Kf4 {His king steps up to f4, your pawn is still on g6, and his rook is free: a draw. [%csl Rg6]}) (56. g3 {Pushing the other pawn does nothing for your runner on g6.} 56... Ra1 {His rook swings to a1, from where it can still get back to the eighth row in time: a draw.}) (56. Bf5+ {The check on f5 puts your bishop right beside his king.} 56... Kxf5 {His king takes the bishop, and his rook and pawn win: you are lost. [%cal Re4f5]}) (56. Kg3 {Bringing your king back to g3 walks into a pin.} 56... Rg5 {His rook pins your bishop to your king along the g column, and the bishop will fall: you are lost. [%cal Rg5g3]}) (56. Bh5 {Guarding the pawn from h5 puts your bishop on the row of his rook.} 56... Rxh5+ {His rook takes the bishop with check. If your king takes back, his e pawn queens as fast as yours: a draw. [%cal Ra5h5]}) 56... Ra8 57. Bf3+ {Check! His king has to step aside, and behind it your bishop sees the rook on a8. [%cal Gf3a8]} (57. Kg5 {Walking your king up instead of checking gives his rook all the time it needs.} 57... Rb8 {His rook holds the eighth row, and your pawn can never get past it: a draw.}) (57. Be2 {On e2 your bishop is nowhere near his rook, so he has time to block your pawn.} 57... Rg8 {His rook parks on g8, in front of your pawn, and you are lost. [%csl Rg8]}) (57. Bf5+ {The check on f5 hands your bishop to his king.} 57... Kxf5 {His king takes the bishop, and his rook still guards the eighth row: you are lost. [%cal Re4f5]}) (57. Bh5 {From h5 your bishop does nothing to his rook on a8.} 57... Rg8 {From g8 his rook stops your pawn for good: you are lost. [%csl Rg8]}) 57... Kd4 58. Bxa8 {The rook falls. Now watch the race to the queens. [%csl Ga8][%tail]} (58. Kg5 {Stepping your king up instead of taking the free rook on a8 lets it live.} 58... Rb8 {His rook keeps the eighth row, and your pawn is stuck: a draw.}) (58. Bg4 {Tucking the bishop back to g4 leaves his rook alive on a8.} 58... Rg8 {His rook reaches g8 and blocks the pawn: you are lost. [%csl Rg8]}) (58. Be4 {Your bishop steps to e4, next to his king, instead of taking the rook on a8.} 58... Kxe4 {His king takes the bishop for free, and you are lost. [%cal Rd4e4]}) 58... e2 59. g8=Q 59... e1=Q+ 60. Kh3 {Queen, bishop and pawn against his lone queen win. The push to g7 dragged his rook to a8, onto your bishop's diagonal; on a7 it would have held the draw.} 1-0


