[FEN "3k4/8/2pb4/7R/1p2K3/8/1PP1nP2/8 b - - 0 43"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3k4/8/2pb4/7R/1p2K3/8/1PP1nP2/8_b_-_-_0_43"]

{A rook against a bishop and a knight. Two minor pieces are hard to beat while they guard each other, so look for a way to split them up.}
43... Nf4 {His knight jumps to f4 and attacks your rook, and that is a mistake: now only his bishop on d6 guards the knight. [%cal Rf4h5,Rd6f4]} 44. Rh6 {Your rook steps out of the attack and hits the bishop on d6, the knight's only guard. [%cal Gh6d6]} (44. Rh4 {Attacking the knight from h4 lets it step back to d5, next to his pawn on c6.} 44... Nd5 45. Kd4 Ke7 {His knight is guarded by the pawn on c6, and his pieces stay together: level.}) (44. Rh8+ {The check on h8 just chases his king to safety.} 44... Kc7 45. Rh7+ Kb6 {His king is safe on b6, and nothing has changed: level.}) (44. Re5 {Offering your rook on e5 lets his bishop swap itself off.} 44... Bxe5 45. Kxe5 Nd5 {Rook for bishop leaves your pawns against his knight and pawns: level.}) (44. Rf5 {Attacking the knight from f5 lets it step back to e6.} 44... Ne6 45. Rf6 Ke7 {His king on e7 guards both his pieces, and the game stays level. [%cal Re7e6,Re7d6]}) 44... Bf8 {His bishop runs to f8 and attacks your rook, but the knight on f4 is now on its own.} 45. Rf6 {Both his pieces stand on the f column, and your rook attacks them both. [%cal Gf6f8,Gf6f4]} (45. Rxc6 {Grabbing the pawn on c6 gives his king time to come and guard the knight.} 45... Kd7 46. Rf6 Ne6 {The knight is safe on e6, next to his king, and the win has gone. [%cal Rd7e6]}) (45. Kxf4 {Your king takes the knight, but your rook on h6 is left hanging.} 45... Bxh6+ {His bishop takes the rook with check, and now you are the one who is lost. [%cal Rf8h6]}) (45. Ke5 {The king steps into the centre and leaves your rook on h6 hanging.} 45... Bxh6 {His bishop takes the rook for nothing: you are lost. [%cal Rf8h6]}) (45. Rh8 {The rook steps out of the attack and pins the bishop on f8, but his knight can come back to guard it.} 45... Ne6 46. Kf5 Kd7 {His king steps up and both his pieces are safe again: level.}) 45... Bg7 46. Rxf4 {The knight falls, and your rook and pawns beat his bishop and pawns, even if the bishop takes on b2. Split two minor pieces apart, and a rook picks them off. [%csl Gf4]} (46. Rxc6 {Taking the pawn on c6 lets the knight on f4 escape.} 46... Nh3 47. f4 Bxb2 {His knight got out and his bishop took your pawn on b2: the win has gone.}) (46. Rf8+ {The check on f8 puts your rook right where his bishop can take it.} 46... Bxf8 {His bishop takes the rook, and now his two pieces beat your pawns: you are lost. [%cal Rg7f8]}) (46. Kf5 {Guarding the rook with your king still lets his bishop swap itself off.} 46... Bxf6 47. Kxf6 Kd7 {You traded your rook for his bishop, and his knight survives: you are lost.}) 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: the bishop drives, the knight forks"]
