[FEN "8/4bk2/4n3/7K/6P1/8/6R1/8 w - - 21 81"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/4bk2/4n3/7K/6P1/8/6R1/8_w_-_-_21_81"]

{Your bishop and knight against his rook and pawn, which is usually a draw. But two minor pieces can work as a team: one drives the king, the other forks.}
81. Kh6 {He walks his king up to h6 to help his pawn forward, and that is a mistake: out in the open, his king can be pushed around with checks.} 81... Bf8+ {The bishop drives his king back with check. [%cal Gf8h6]} (81... Nf4 {Attacking the rook straight away, with no check, lets it simply step aside.} 82. Rg1 {The rook is safe on g1, and there is nothing left to fork: a draw.}) (81... Bg5+ {This check puts your bishop on g5, where his king can attack it.} 82. Kh5 {His king now attacks your bishop, so a knight fork on f4 would leave the bishop hanging: a draw. [%csl Rg5]}) (81... Bd6 {Pulling the bishop back to d6 gives up the check and gives his pawn time.} 82. g5 {His pawn gets going, and the fork never comes: a draw.}) (81... Nd4 {Sending the knight toward g2 the long way, through d4, is too slow.} 82. g5 {His pawn runs while your knight is still on its way: a draw.}) 82. Kh5 82... Nf4+ {The fork: check to his king, and the rook on g2 is attacked too. [%cal Gf4h5,Gf4g2]} (82... Ng7+ {Checking with the knight from g7 leaves the rook on g2 alone.} 83. Kh4 {His king steps to h4, and your knight on g7 cannot reach the rook: a draw.}) (82... Kf6 {Walking your king to f6 instead of forking wastes the moment.} 83. g5+ {His pawn checks you from g5, and the fork is gone: a draw.}) (82... Bd6 {Retreating the bishop to d6 lets his rook get away.} 83. Rg1 {His rook tucks into g1, and nothing is attacked: a draw.}) (82... Nd4 {Heading for the rook the slow way, through d4, gives him time.} 83. g5 {His pawn runs on while your knight travels: a draw.}) 83. Kh4 83... Nxg2+ {The rook falls with check, and bishop and knight beat his lone pawn. Your bishop drove his king to h5, where your knight could fork it with the rook. [%csl Gg2]} (83... Ng6+ {Another check, from g6, instead of taking the rook on g2 lets it live.} 84. Kg3 {His king steps up to guard the rook: a draw.}) (83... Be7+ {The bishop check from e7 leaves the rook on g2 untouched.} 84. Kg3 {His king steps up beside his rook, and both are safe: a draw.}) (83... Kf6 {Bringing your king to f6 instead of taking the rook lets it survive.} 84. g5+ {His pawn checks you, and his rook on g2 is still alive: a draw.}) 0-1


