[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/5k1p/2P5/1B6/1p3Pp1/1N3p2/7r/5K2_b_-_-_0_43"]

{Your bishop, knight and passed pawn against his rook and a crowd of pawns. He is winning: his pawns on the king side are close to queening.}
43... Ke6 {His king steps to e6 to help stop your c pawn, and that is his mistake: he trusts his rook to catch the pawn and misses a knight fork.} 44. c7 {His rook has to stop the pawn, and it can only do that from c2. [%cal Gc7c8]} (44. Nd4+ {The knight check comes one move early: his rook is not on c2 yet.} 44... Kd6 45. Ba4 Rd2 {His rook attacks your knight on d4, and his king sits next to your pawn: you are lost.}) (44. Nc5+ {The check from c5 does nothing about his rook.} 44... Kd6 45. Ne4+ Kc7 {His king reaches c7, right in front of your pawn: you are lost.}) (44. f5+ {The pawn check on f5 gains nothing.} 44... Kd6 45. Nc5 Rc2 {His rook reaches c2 in time and hits your knight: you are lost.}) (44. Bc4+ {The bishop check from c4 has nothing behind it.} 44... Kd6 45. Nd4 Rd2 {His rook gets to d2 first and attacks your knight: you are lost.}) (44. Be2 {Blocking his f pawn with the bishop on e2 just feeds the bishop to the pawn.} 44... fxe2+ {His pawn takes the bishop with check, and you are lost. [%cal Rf3e2]}) 44... Rc2 45. Nd4+ {Check from d4, and the same knight hits the rook on c2: a fork. [%cal Gd4e6,Gd4c2]} (45. c8=Q {A new queen on c8, but his rook on c2 has been watching that square all along. [%cal Rc2c8]} 45... Rxc8 {His rook takes the queen, and you are lost.}) (45. Bd3 {Hitting the rook with the bishop from d3 lets it take your pawn instead.} 45... Rxc7 {His rook takes the pawn on c7, and you are lost.}) (45. Bc6 {Blocking the column with the bishop on c6 just hangs the bishop.} 45... Rxc6 {His rook takes the bishop, and the pawn falls next: you are lost. [%cal Rc2c6]}) (45. Nd2 {Attacking his f3 pawn with the knight ignores his rook.} 45... Rxc7 {His rook takes your pawn on c7, and the whole plan is gone: you are lost.}) 45... Kd6 46. Nxc2 {The rook falls, and your bishop and knight beat his pawns. The pawn push forced his rook onto c2, the one square where your knight could fork it with his king. [%csl Gc2]} (46. Nf5+ {A second check, from f5, instead of taking the rook lets it live.} 46... Kxc7 {His king takes your pawn on c7 and keeps his rook: you are lost.}) (46. Bd3 {Adding the bishop's attack from d3, instead of taking the rook, gives it time to escape.} 46... Rc1+ 47. Kf2 Kxc7 {His rook escapes with check and his king eats your pawn: you are lost.}) (46. Be8 {Tucking the bishop away on e8 leaves the rook free to run.} 46... Rc1+ 47. Kf2 Kxc7 {His rook gets out with check and his king takes your pawn: you are losing.}) 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: let him have the queen"]
[Result "0-1"]
