[FEN "8/8/2P1r3/5p2/1P5N/5k2/3K4/8 b - - 3 50"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2P1r3/5p2/1P5N/5k2/3K4/8_b_-_-_3_50"]

{Your knight and two pawns against his rook and pawn. Two passed pawns close together can be worth more than a whole piece.}
50... Kg4 {His king goes after your knight on h4, and that is a mistake: it takes his king far away from your pawns.} 51. b5 {Let the knight go! The pawn on b5 guards the one on c6, and together they are worth more than the knight. [%cal Gb5c6]} (51. Nf3 {Saving the knight on f3 does not work: his king takes it there anyway.} 51... Kxf3 {The knight is gone all the same, and your pawns have lost a move: a draw.}) (51. Nxf5 {Taking the pawn on f5 puts the knight right beside his king.} 51... Kxf5 {His king takes the knight, and his rook beats your two pawns: you are lost. [%cal Rg4f5]}) (51. c7 {Pushing the c pawn first lets his rook come to c6, behind your pawn.} 51... Rc6 {His rook attacks the pawn on c7 from behind, and your b pawn is too far back to help: you are lost.}) (51. Kc3 {Your king on c3 does nothing for the pawn on c6, which his rook is attacking.} 51... Rxc6+ {His rook takes your pawn on c6 with check: you are lost. [%cal Re6c6]}) (51. Ng6 {Saving the knight on g6 costs your pawns their move.} 51... f4 {His pawn runs, and the chance has gone: a draw.}) 51... Kxh4 {He takes the knight. Now, which pawn goes first?} 52. c7 (52. b6 {The back pawn first leaves the front pawn on c6 unguarded.} 52... Rxc6 {His rook takes the pawn on c6, and you are lost. [%cal Re6c6]}) (52. Kc3 {The king walk to c3 gives his pawn a free move.} 52... f4 {His pawn is now quick enough to race yours: a draw.}) (52. Kd3 {Your king steps to d3, and his pawn gets a free move.} 52... f4 {This time his pawn wins the race: you are lost.}) 52... Re8 53. b6 {Your pawns stand on the sixth and seventh rows, and his rook cannot stop both. [%csl Gb6,Gc7][%tail]} (53. c8=Q {A queen on c8 is taken at once: his rook on e8 guards that square. [%cal Re8c8]} 53... Rxc8 {His rook takes the new queen, and you are lost.}) (53. Kc3 {Waiting with your king on c3 hands his pawn a tempo.} 53... f4 {His pawn gets moving, and it is only a draw.}) (53. Kd3 {Stepping to d3 gives his pawn the move it needs.} 53... f4 {His pawn now queens first: you are lost.}) 53... Rc8 54. b7 54... Rxc7 55. b8=Q {A new queen on b8. Two passed pawns side by side beat a rook, because the rook can only stop one of them, and that is why you could let the knight go. [%csl Gb8]} 1-0


