{Your bishop and pawns against his rook and pawns, and his a pawn is one step from a queen. In a race like this, checks buy time.}
52... Re1 {He attacks your bishop on e5 instead of making his queen, and that is his mistake. His king on e8 is the only thing guarding d8. [%cal Re8d8]} 53. d7+ (53. Bc3 {Stopping his a pawn with the bishop on c3 gives up the race.} 53... a1=Q 54. Bxa1 Rxa1 {Your bishop is gone for his pawn, and his rook stops your pawns: you are lost.}) (53. Bf6 {Your bishop goes to f6 without a check, and he has time to take your pawn on e6.} 53... Rxe6 {His rook takes your pawn on e6, and you are lost.}) (53. Kd5 {Stepping your king to d5 leaves the bishop on e5 hanging.} 53... Rxe5+ 54. Kxe5 {You get his rook for your bishop, but nothing can stop his a pawn now: you are lost.}) (53. f4 {A quiet pawn move leaves the bishop on e5 hanging.} 53... Rxe5+ {His rook takes the bishop with check, and you are lost. [%cal Re1e5]}) 53... Ke7 {His king steps to e7 and still guards d8. [%cal Re7d8]} 54. Bf6+ {Check! Wherever his king goes now, it can no longer guard d8. [%cal Gf6e7][%csl Gd8]} (54. d8=Q+ {A new queen on d8, but his king stands right next to her.} 54... Kxd8 {His king takes the queen, and you are lost. [%cal Re7d8]}) (54. Bd4 {The bishop on d4 watches a1, but gives no check. [%cal Gd4a1]} 54... Rxe6 {His rook takes your pawn on e6 while nothing threatens him: you are lost.}) (54. Bc7 {Guarding d8 from c7 is too slow without a check. [%cal Gc7d8]} 54... Rd1 {His rook gets behind your d pawn, and you are lost.}) (54. Kd5 {Bringing your king forward to d5 gives his a pawn its move.} 54... a1=Q 55. Bxa1 Rxa1 {Your bishop had to give itself up for his new queen, and now his rook wins: you are lost.}) 54... Kxf6 55. d8=Q+ {A new queen on d8, with check. Now watch her chase his king. [%csl Gd8][%tail]} (55. Kd6 {Escorting the pawn with your king gives him time.} 55... Rd1+ 56. Kc7 a1=Q {His rook checks first, and his a pawn queens before yours: you are lost.}) (55. Kxb5 {Grabbing the pawn on b5 instead of queening gives his king time.} 55... Ke7 {His king gets back to e7 to guard d8, and his a pawn is still one step away: you are lost. [%cal Re7d8]}) (55. f4 {A quiet pawn move lets his king come back.} 55... Ke7 {His king returns to e7, and your pawn on d7 is stuck: you are lost. [%cal Re7d8]}) 55... Kg7 56. Qc7+ {While you keep checking, his a pawn cannot move.} 56... Kh6 57. Qf4+ 57... Kh7 58. Qf7+ 58... Kh8 59. Qh5+ 59... Kg8 60. Qxg4+ {Your queen takes the g pawn with check: queen and three pawns against his rook and two. Your bishop gave itself up to drag his king off d8.} 1-0


