{Two rooks against his rook, knight and five pawns, and his h pawn is one step from queening. You are losing the race, unless something else ends the game first.}
46... b3 {He pushes his b pawn to race for a second queen, and that is a mistake: he has forgotten about his own king on a5.} 47. Ra1+ {Check down the a column! Only his knight can block it. [%cal Ga1a5]} (47. Rxb6 {Taking the knight on b6 lets his rook check you from c7.} 47... Rc7+ 48. Rc6 Rxc6+ 49. Kxc6 {The rooks come off, and your last rook cannot stop two of his pawns: you are lost.}) (47. Rxh2 {Taking the h pawn lets his rook take yours.} 47... Rxh2 {His rook takes back on h2, and he is a knight up: you are lost. [%cal Rh7h2]}) (47. Rd1 {Moving your rook off h1 lets his h pawn through.} 47... h1=Q {His pawn queens on h1, and you are lost.}) (47. Rxg6 {Grabbing the pawn on g6 gives his rook time to check.} 47... Rc7+ 48. Kd6 Rc2 {His rook checks you away and swings to c2, and his pawns are too fast: you are lost.}) 47... Na4+ 48. Rxa4+ {A rook for his knight, with check, and his king has to take back.} (48. Kc4 {Stepping out of the check to c4 gives up the rook sacrifice.} 48... Rh4+ 49. Kxb3 h1=Q {His h pawn queens, and you are lost.}) (48. Kd6 {Running your king to d6 instead of taking the knight gives up the attack.} 48... h1=Q {His pawn queens on h1, and mate was one move away for you: you are lost.}) (48. Kd5 {Stepping to d5 instead of taking the knight ignores his h pawn.} 48... h1=Q+ {His pawn queens with check, and you are lost.}) 48... Kxa4 49. Rxa6# {Mate on a6! You gave a rook for a knight and got the one thing that counts. He raced his pawns home and never looked at his own king. [%cal Ga6a4]} (49. Rb6 {Close, but the mate is on the a column, not on b6.} 49... a5 {His pawn steps to a5, the mate is gone, and his pawns win: you are lost.}) (49. Kc4 {Forgetting the mate and stepping to c4 lets his attack roll.} 49... Rh4+ 50. Kc5 b2 {His rook checks you back and his b pawn is one step from queening: you are lost.}) (49. Rxg6 {Grabbing a pawn on g6 instead of mating on a6 lets him off.} 49... Rc7+ 50. Kd4 h1=Q {His rook checks you away and his h pawn queens: you are lost.}) 1-0


