66. Rh8 {His rook goes to h8, and he thinks it is safe there because your own pawn on d4 blocks your bishop's diagonal. That is his mistake: pawns can move. [%cal Gc3h8]} 66... e2 (66... Kf3 {Walking your king to f3 lets his rook check you from f8.} 67. Rf8+ {His rook checks you from f8 and pushes your king back: the chance has gone.}) (66... Bb2 {A quiet bishop move gives his h pawn a free check.} 67. h3+ {His pawn checks you from h3, and your pawns still block the bishop: the moment has passed.}) (66... Be1 {On e1 your bishop no longer looks at h8. [%cal Gc3h8]} 67. h3+ {The check on h3 pushes your king around, and his rook on h8 is out of danger.}) (66... Bd2 {Guarding the e pawn from d2 pulls your bishop off the road to h8. [%cal Gc3h8]} 67. Rc8 {His rook swings to c8, safe, and the chance has gone for good.}) 67. Kxe2 67... d3+ {Check, and the diagonal from c3 to h8 is open: his rook is hanging. [%cal Gc3h8]} (67... Ba5 {The bishop was never in danger on c3, and on a5 it leaves the diagonal to h8.} 68. Kd3 {His king steps in front of your d pawn, so it can no longer check: you are lost.}) (67... Kf4 {Bringing your king to f4 does nothing while the diagonal is still blocked.} 68. Rf8+ {His rook checks you from f8, and the chance is gone: you are lost.}) (67... Be1 {Offering the bishop on e1 hands it to his king.} 68. Kxe1 {His king collects the bishop on e1: you are lost. [%cal Re2e1]}) (67... Bd2 {The bishop on d2 stands right next to his king.} 68. Kxd2 {His king takes the bishop on d2, and you are lost.}) 68. Kxd3 68... Bxh8 {Your bishop takes the rook on h8. Two pawns bought a whole rook: they cleared your bishop's diagonal, the second one with check. [%csl Gh8]} (68... Be5 {The bishop on e5 looks fine, but the rook in the corner was the prize.} 69. h3+ {His pawn checks you and his rook gets away: you are lost.}) (68... Kf4 {Your king steps to f4 and forgets that his king attacks your bishop on c3.} 69. Kxc3 {His king takes your bishop on c3, and you are lost. [%cal Rd3c3]}) (68... Bb4 {The bishop on b4 leaves his rook alive.} 69. c3 {His pawn attacks the bishop, and his rook is still on h8: you are lost.}) 0-1


