41... Nxb3 {His knight grabs your pawn on b3, and that is greedy: it takes the knight far away from the king side, where the danger is.} 42. g4 {Two squares in one go! From g4 your pawn can check on g5 next move. [%csl Gg4]} (42. g3 {One square instead of two costs a whole tempo.} 42... Nd2 {His knight gets back in time, and your pawns are stopped: you are lost.}) (42. h7 {Pushing the h pawn before the check lets his king step in front of it.} 42... Kg7 {His king on g7 guards h8 and attacks your pawn: you are lost. [%cal Rg7h8]}) (42. Kg4 {The king walk to g4 is too slow for this race.} 42... a4 {His a pawn is simply quicker: you are lost.}) (42. Kh4 {Stepping the king to h4 is just as slow.} 42... a4 {His a pawn runs, and your king is nowhere near it: you are lost.}) 42... a4 43. g5+ (43. h7 {Pushing the h pawn one move early lets his king catch it.} 43... Kg7 {His king reaches g7, next to your pawn and its queening square: you are lost. [%cal Rg7h8]}) (43. Kh4 {A quiet king move wastes the tempo you need.} 43... Nd4 {Without the check, his knight gets back in time: you are lost.}) 43... Kxf5 44. h7 {His king took the f pawn, and now nothing can reach your h pawn. [%csl Gh7][%tail]} (44. g6 {Pushing the g pawn instead of the h pawn lets his king stop both.} 44... Kf6 {His king on f6 holds both pawns back, and you are lost.}) (44. Kh4 {Waiting with the king costs the tempo you do not have.} 44... Kg6 {His king gets in front of your h pawn, and you are lost.}) 44... Nd4 45. h8=Q {A new queen on h8, and his knight never came close. The double step to g4 let the check on g5 come in time, and his knight was far away on the other wing. [%csl Gh8]} 1-0


